zxkit-mc for Debian
-------------------

Where the files are
  /usr/lib/mc/extfs.d/zxkit     the extfs helper, next to Midnight Commander's own
                                (uzip, urar, ...), so MC finds it without any setup
  /usr/share/zxkit/mc/zxkit.ini the file associations, in the mc.ext.ini 4.0 format
  /usr/bin/zxkit-mc-setup       adds those associations to a user's configuration

Without any setup
  In a Midnight Commander panel, type:  cd /path/to/games.tap/zxkit://
  The image is then an ordinary directory: copying files in and out (F5), making
  directories (F7) and deleting files (F8) rewrite the image.

Enter and F3 on images
  Run this once as each user who wants Enter to open images and F3 to list them:

      zxkit-mc-setup

  Then restart Midnight Commander. "zxkit-mc-setup --remove" undoes it.

Why this is not done system-wide
  Midnight Commander reads a single extension file. The system one,
  /etc/mc/mc.ext.ini, is a configuration file of the mc package, which another
  package must not edit, and there is no drop-in directory for it. A user's
  ~/.config/mc/mc.ext.ini replaces the system file completely, so
  zxkit-mc-setup starts from a copy of /etc/mc/mc.ext.ini when the user has no
  file, and otherwise only adds or replaces its own marked block. It refuses to
  touch a file that is not in the 4.0 format (MC then reports "The format of the
  ... mc.ext.ini file has changed with version 4.0"): move that file away and run
  it again.

  Debian releases with Midnight Commander older than 4.8.29 (for example
  bookworm) use the old mc.ext format and cannot use the associations, so the
  package depends on a newer mc.
